<h2 id="summary">Executive summary</h2>
<p>Large power transformers are slow to procure. The evidence points to pressure on manufacturing and on several qualified inputs, including core steel, bushings and tap changers. A global ranking of those constraints would require data on deliverable output and orders for comparable specifications. Customs evidence measures a different kind of exposure. In the EU, China’s share of imported transformers was much larger than its share on a value-based apparent-supply calculation. The transformer trade premium also depends on which equipment serves as the comparison group. These results support tracking supplier exposure, procurement pressure and project timing directly.</p>
<p><b>Added 25 November 2026.</b> Official statistics and company accounts now quantify part of that pressure. The order backlog of the German branch that makes motors, generators and transformers covers ${de_2711_latest} months of sales, against ${de_2711_2019} at the end of 2019. The order books of four large makers equal ${ratio_lo} to ${ratio_hi} years of segment revenue. Where transformer output is counted in units, in Japan and the EU, the counts show no rise. Electrical steel is a different case: in the EU there was no shortage in total tonnage in 2025, with mills at ${steel_util_2025} of capacity while imports rose, but the highest grades come from imports and the figures do not show whether those were tight.</p>
<p>This paper considers large power transformers used in transmission, with European and US procurement evidence. The <a href="../grid-exposure">companion paper on supplier exposure</a> compares wider customs baskets. Their ratings and end uses are broader than the transmission equipment discussed here.</p>

<h2 id="manufacturing">Several input routes converge at assembly</h2>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="network-figure">
  <h3 id="network-figure">Transformer manufacturing and delivery</h3>
  <div class="branches">
    <div class="hop"><div class="n">Core route</div><div class="t">Steel inputs → grain-oriented electrical steel → slitting and core production.</div></div>
    <div class="hop"><div class="n">Winding route</div><div class="t">Copper or aluminium production and refining → conductor preparation and winding.</div></div>
    <div class="hop"><div class="n">Other qualified inputs</div><div class="t">Bushings, tap changers, insulation, tank, oil and cooling equipment.</div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="network-join"><b>Factory.</b> Incoming inputs converge in core and winding integration, drying and insulation, final assembly and factory acceptance testing.</p>
  <p class="network-join"><b>Project site.</b> Heavy transport, installation and commissioning proceed when equipment, site and network work are ready.</p>
  <figcaption class="note">The branches supply the same assembly process. The product specification determines which designs, grades and suppliers can serve an order. <a href="#source-entso">ENTSO-E, pp. 15–16</a> and <a href="#source-nlr">National Laboratory of the Rockies, Table 3.</a></figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Public trade data distinguish some finished products and inputs. They leave the grade, specification, approved supplier, factory slot and input origin inside imported equipment unresolved. Copper and aluminium are both relevant to this network, with substitution depending on equipment design and qualification. A broad measure of metal production leaves their deliverability to a particular project unknown.</p>

<h2 id="trade-evidence">Core-steel trade is concentrated</h2>
<p>Grain-oriented electrical steel (GOES) is the magnetic steel used in conventional transformer cores. From 2019 to 2024, China’s share of world GOES export value rose from ${global_start} to ${global_end}. It supplied ${global_mass_end} of recorded tonnes in 2024. The 2022–2024 pooled exporter HHI was ${pooled_hhi} for GOES and ${transformer_hhi} for liquid-dielectric transformers. HHI runs from zero to one in this comparison. The global calculation uses the same source, time window and value basis for both products. <a href="#source-baci">CEPII BACI, HS17 V202601.</a></p>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="goes-figure">
  <h3 id="goes-figure">China’s share of GOES exports and EU imports</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">World exports · value</th><th scope="col">World exports · tonnes</th><th scope="col">EU imports · value</th><th scope="col">EU imports · tonnes</th><th scope="col">World HHI · value</th><th scope="col">World HHI · tonnes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>${trends}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <figcaption class="note">World exports follow HS17 lines 722511 and 722611. EU imports use their CN8 equivalents, extra-EU27 excluding the United Kingdom. BACI flow tonnage can be estimated. Rows with positive mass account for ${mass_coverage} of 2024 export value. The world series ends with the BACI release’s 2024 data. <a href="#source-baci">CEPII</a> and <a href="#source-comext">Eurostat.</a></figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Both value and tonnage show rising Chinese shares. Value and mass give different rankings for some exporters because prices vary and the basket combines grades and widths. The 2024 value HHI is ${wide_hhi} for sheet at least 600 mm wide and ${narrow_hhi} for narrower strip. Customs records also combine original sale and re-export channels. Domestic sales, product mix and re-exports can push production concentration above or below export concentration.</p>
<p class="note"><b>Correction, 24 November 2026.</b> The previous Grid Chain called its export HHI a lower bound on production concentration. The trade data support no directional bound. The 2022–2024 HHI is calculated after pooling annual export amounts. The published value uses pooled shares.</p>

<h3 id="exposure">EU production changes the supplier exposure estimate</h3>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="exposure-figure">
  <h4 id="exposure-figure">China’s share of EU imports and estimated apparent supply</h4>
  <div class="pairs">${pairs}</div>
  <figcaption class="note">2024, by value. Both measures use gross imports from China in the numerator. Supply adds EU sold production to extra-EU imports and subtracts extra-EU exports. The <a href="../grid-exposure">companion paper on supplier exposure</a> gives the method, product definitions and sensitivity ranges.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>China supplied 34.2% of liquid-dielectric transformer imports and 9.1% on the value-based apparent-supply measure. For GOES the corresponding value shares were 51.9% and 24.2%. China’s share of EU GOES import value rose from ${eu_start} in 2019 to ${eu_end} in 2025. These customs baskets are wider than transmission equipment: the transformer lines run from small distribution units to large power transformers. The aggregates leave the geography of qualified substitutes and the origin of steel in imported transformers unresolved. Supplier exposure and scarcity are separate questions, and the <a href="../grid-exposure">companion paper</a> treats the first. <a href="#source-prodcom">Eurostat Prodcom</a> and <a href="#source-comext">Comext.</a></p>
<p>Direct US exposure to the same exporting country is much smaller. In the September study’s 2024 US customs data, China accounted for ${us_goes_china} of GOES import value and ${us_transformer_china} of liquid-dielectric transformer import value. Japan and South Korea supplied most US GOES imports. The national laboratory’s US study identifies heavy reliance on imported high-grade steel for large power transformers. A low share from China can coexist with substantial import reliance on other origins. <a href="#source-trade">September trade study, section 4.7</a> and <a href="#source-nlr">National Laboratory of the Rockies, Table 4.</a></p>

<h3>The trade comparison depends on the equipment selected</h3>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="unit-value-figure">
  <h4 id="unit-value-figure">Relative transformer customs value per tonne</h4>
  ${forest_rows}
  <figcaption class="note">2023–2024 versus 2012–2020. Each point and its 95% interval transform the reported log coefficient to a percentage. The vertical marker shows zero. Source is the September Atlas study, BACI HS02, using flow and year effects and tariff-line clustered inference. Liquid-dielectric transformers spanning several ratings and uses are pooled in the studied transformer codes. <a href="#source-trade">September 2026 trade study.</a></figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The larger premium appears against the heavy-machinery basket. The interval against motors, pumps and compressors includes zero. A separate transformer-specific shock remains unresolved. Re-estimating both from the original BACI flows reproduced the September estimates and intervals. The filed pre-trend rule also fails, so the estimates remain descriptive. Changes in the type and rating of equipment traded can move value per tonne independently of contract prices. The IEA survey and customs estimates can therefore report different changes for different baskets.</p>

<h2 id="procurement">Long waits are documented across markets</h2>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="lead-time-figure">
  <h3 id="lead-time-figure">Large transformer procurement evidence</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Market</th><th scope="col">Survey or reporting scope</th><th scope="col">Lead time</th><th scope="col">Related observations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>${market}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <figcaption class="note">These observations use different dates, equipment definitions and summary statistics. The IEA surveyed industry stakeholders in 2024 and reported record manufacturer backlogs for that year. The published charts do not provide an item-level survey dataset. The ENTSO-E 2026 pipeline survey received data from 27 European transmission operators. The IEA identifies permitting as the primary reported source of transmission delays, especially in advanced economies.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Demand growth, existing orders and availability of qualified inputs can jointly lengthen lead times. ENTSO-E’s planned additions help define the European demand calendar and it reports limited visibility into manufacturing capacity. The US laboratory report identifies long component lead times from manufacturer interviews. Investment announcements report planned expansion. Deliverable capacity requires a dated picture of operational output, staffing, testing slots, key inputs, uncommitted orders, transport and eligible suppliers. The public benchmarks do not allocate the order delays to one stage.</p>

<h2 id="orders">Order books and prices rose faster than output</h2>
<p><b>Added 25 November 2026.</b> No public statistic measures deliverable transformer capacity by rating. Several do measure order backlogs, prices and output for the industries and companies that make transformers. Only the US price index, the Japanese series and the EU production series are specific to transformers. The German and US backlog figures cover a wider group of electrical equipment.</p>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="backlog-figure">
  <h3 id="backlog-figure">Order backlogs and prices in official statistics</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Industry covered</th><th scope="col">End of 2019</th><th scope="col">Latest</th><th scope="col">Comparison, same dates</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>${backlog_rows}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <figcaption class="note">The German figure is the order backlog divided by a twelve-month average of the branch’s sales. The US figure is unfilled orders divided by average monthly shipments over the previous twelve months, calculated here from the published series. Price indices are rebased here to their 2019 average. The latest German figures are provisional and the latest US price figures are preliminary. ${src_de} ${src_us} ${src_ppi}</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>In Germany the order backlog of the branch that makes motors, generators and transformers covered ${de_2711_latest} months of sales in ${de_latest_label}, against ${de_2711_2019} months at the end of 2019. All manufacturing stood at ${de_c_latest} months. In the United States, unfilled orders for electrical equipment rose from ${us_2019} months of shipments at the end of 2019 to ${us_peak} months at the end of 2022 and were ${us_latest} months in ${us_latest_label}, a rise of ${us_change}. For all manufacturing industries that report unfilled orders the same measure went from ${us_mfg_2019} to ${us_mfg_latest} months, a rise of ${us_mfg_change}. Electrical equipment has a shorter order book than that average, and it has lengthened more. The US producer price index for transformer manufacturing was ${ppi_latest} in ${ppi_latest_label} on a 2019 base of 100, against ${ppi_mfg} for all manufacturing, a relative rise of ${ppi_relative}.</p>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="output-figure">
  <h3 id="output-figure">Orders and output of transformers</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Earlier period</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Later period</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>${output_rows}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <figcaption class="note">Japanese fiscal years start in April. Japanese orders are a survey of 13 manufacturers, by value, for all made-to-order transformers; the published workbooks start in fiscal 2020. The Japanese unit count covers one class, oil-immersed units of 100 MVA and above. The EU 2025 figures come from a newer Prodcom dataset than the 2021 figures, and the unit counts for 2018 to 2020 are not usable. The US index covers electrical equipment as a group. ${src_jp} ${src_eu} ${src_fed}</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>In Japan the value of transformer orders rose by ${jp_orders_change} between fiscal 2020 and fiscal 2025. Units of 100 MVA and above produced averaged ${jp_large_late} a year in fiscal 2022 to 2025, against ${jp_large_early} a year in fiscal 2015 to 2018. Over the years with order data they went from ${jp_large_2020} in fiscal 2020 to ${jp_large_2025} in fiscal 2025, after a low of ${jp_large_2024} in fiscal 2024. In the EU, the value of sold production of transformers above 10 MVA rose by ${eu_value_change} between 2021 and 2025 while the number of units was almost unchanged, at ${eu_units_2021} and ${eu_units_2025}. Value per unit went from EUR ${eu_value_per_unit_2021} million to EUR ${eu_value_per_unit_2025} million. A unit count does not show size, so larger units can explain part of that rise, and so can higher prices. Neither series shows unit output rising. Large units take years to build, so recent orders would not yet appear in output.</p>
<p>The European Commission’s business survey asks manufacturers of electrical equipment what limits their production. The share naming a shortage of material or equipment was ${sv_first} at the start of 2015, peaked at ${sv_peak} in ${sv_peak_q}, and was ${sv_latest} in ${sv_latest_q}. In that quarter ${sv_labour} named labour, ${sv_demand} named demand and ${sv_none} named no limit. Capacity utilisation in the same industry was ${sv_util}, close to its level at the start of 2015. The survey covers all electrical equipment, and on it weak demand is still named more often than supply limits. ${src_sv}</p>

<h3 id="makers">Manufacturers’ order books</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Company and reporting segment</th><th scope="col">Unit</th><th scope="col">Backlog, first year shown</th><th scope="col">Backlog in years of revenue</th><th scope="col">Backlog, latest full year</th><th scope="col">Backlog in years of revenue</th><th scope="col">Backlog growth</th><th scope="col">What the segment contains</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>${firm_rows}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="note">Year-end order backlog and annual revenue as printed in each company’s results documents, linked in the <a href="firm_inputs.json">company figures file</a>. Every segment is wider than transformers. Segment definitions differ between companies, so the rows are not comparable with each other. A ratio is formed only where backlog and revenue are printed on the same basis.</p>
<p>At the latest full-year close, the order backlog of the four segments with a comparable ratio equalled ${ratio_lo} to ${ratio_hi} years of segment revenue. For the two with figures back to 2021 it was about one and a half years then. HD Hyundai Electric’s backlog reached USD ${hd_backlog_latest} million at ${hd_backlog_latest_date}. Backlogs printed by the companies in round billions are marked “about”. Two makers describe parts of their order books in their own words. ${statements}</p>
<p>The same companies have announced new capacity. Most announcements state an investment or a number of jobs and give no capacity figure; Hitachi Energy, for example, announced USD 1.5 billion and then a further USD 4.5 billion of investment in 2024 without one. Those that state a capacity figure are listed here.</p>
<ul>
${announcements}
</ul>
<p>One study has counted capacity plant by plant. It covers ${pc_facilities} factories in ${pc_countries} countries making transformers above 10 MVA, and reports that demand grew by ${pc_demand} from 2019 while nameplate capacity grew by less than ${pc_capacity}, with ${pc_additions} GVA of additions identified for 2024 to 2027. It also reports that total nameplate capacity is above estimated demand, and offers two explanations: plants run below nameplate, or its model overestimates plant capacity. It is an industry analyst’s article in a trade journal. ${pc_link}</p>

<h3 id="steel-supply">Electrical steel in the EU: imports rose while mills ran below capacity</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Union production, tonnes</th><th scope="col">Union capacity, tonnes</th><th scope="col">Capacity use</th><th scope="col">Imports of electrical steel, tonnes</th><th scope="col">Union producers’ share of consumption</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>${steel_rows}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="note">Grain-oriented electrical steel. Union figures are from the questionnaire replies of the two Union producers. The share of consumption counts imports of steel laminations and cores as well as steel. ${steel_link}</p>
<p>The European Commission adopted a provisional safeguard measure on imports of grain-oriented electrical steel, and of laminations and cores made from it, on 18 September 2026. It entered into force on ${steel_in_force}. Its preliminary investigation found that Union mills used ${steel_util_2025} of their capacity in 2025, down from ${steel_util_2021} in 2021, while imports of the steel grew ${steel_imports_multiple} times and the Union producers’ share of consumption fell from ${steel_share_2021} to ${steel_share_2025}. China’s share of imports of the steel and of cores made from it rose from ${steel_cn_2021} to ${steel_cn_2025}. The Commission estimates existing world overcapacity at more than ${steel_overcapacity} tonnes. One of the two Union producers closed both its plants for the second half of December 2025, ran its French plant at half capacity from January 2026, and announced a further closure for June to September 2026. ${steel_shutdown_link}</p>
<p>These figures show no shortage of electrical steel in total tonnage in the EU in 2025: imports more than doubled from 2021 and Union mills ran at ${steel_util_2025} of capacity. The same regulation is specific about grades. It records that “${steel_q49}” It also found that Union producers could not make the highest quality needed for the most efficient transformers, and the producers agreed. So the idle capacity is in grades the mills make. The figures do not show whether the highest grades, which come from imports, were tight. The concentration of this trade, set out above, is an exposure to particular suppliers, most of all for those grades.</p>

<h2 id="project">A faster transformer helps when it changes the finish date</h2>
<figure class="panel" aria-labelledby="project-figure">
  <h3 id="project-figure">Completion under earlier equipment delivery</h3>
  <p class="note"><span class="conf snapshot">Modelled</span> All times are illustrative months from project start.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Project case</th><th scope="col">Permit and site work</th><th scope="col">Transformer arrival</th><th scope="col">Network ready</th><th scope="col">Original finish</th><th scope="col">Finish with earlier transformer</th><th scope="col">Months saved</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>${project_rows}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <figcaption class="note">Site work starts after permitting. Procurement and linked network work can proceed in parallel. Installation and commissioning take four months after all prerequisites are ready. The model moves the same qualified transformer’s arrival six months earlier and calculates the change in energization time.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Earlier equipment delivery shortens the transformer-led schedule. It leaves the permit-led schedule unchanged, and has no effect when site work shares the last completion date. Faster delivery can still reduce risk from unexpected equipment delay. An actual project schedule would need dates for the permit, transport, construction, installation and network dependencies.</p>

<h2 id="methods">Evidence needed to identify a bottleneck</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Test</th><th scope="col">Finding here</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Is trade concentrated?</th><td>Compare exporter shares across the same period and basis. Recompute by value and tonnes.</td><td>Observed for GOES. Its pooled value HHI exceeds that of the transformer basket.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Are buyers facing procurement pressure?</th><td>Check lead times, backlogs, quoted prices and buyer access using defined equipment categories.</td><td>Documented for large transformers. Some specialised inputs also have long waits. Order backlogs at four large makers equal ${ratio_lo} to ${ratio_hi} years of segment revenue, and one maker reports filled production slots for one region.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Is qualified output short?</th><td>For a specification, market and period, divide positive unmet demand by demand. Count only deliverable, eligible production after existing commitments and constraints.</td><td>Partly answered. For electrical steel in the EU there was no shortage in total tonnage in 2025: mills used ${steel_util_2025} of capacity. The highest grades come from imports and are not measured separately. For transformers, unit counts in the EU (above 10 MVA) and Japan (100 MVA and above) show no rise while order values and prices rose. That is consistent with constrained output, and also with larger units, higher prices and the lag between order and delivery. Demand and available capacity by grade and rating remain unpublished.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Would another unit or earlier delivery speed up the project?</th><td>Relax the candidate limit with other dependencies fixed, then recalculate output or completion.</td><td>Demonstrated only in the stated model. It does not assign the frequency of transformer-led projects.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Agreement on delivery dates is the relevant test for a project. Evidence on tooling, skilled labor, acceptance testing, finished transformer inventory and qualified components is needed before attributing the industry’s waits to any single stage. For the EU exposure measure, sold production omits internal use, and the customs and factory-gate values differ. Inventories, changes in the product mix and re-exports matter. The share covers direct gross exposure and cannot identify final absorption or upstream materials embedded in equipment.</p>

<h2 id="trade">Customs context for conductors and transformers</h2>
<p>The Atlas slider covers the frozen codes for liquid-dielectric transformers above 650 and up to 10,000 kVA and copper wire above 6 mm. The dedicated GOES and transformer studies above use their separately documented baskets. The slider provides goods-trade context.</p>
<div class="panel">
  <div class="control" id="static-trade" hidden><label class="note" for="trade-slider">Trade year</label><input id="trade-slider" type="range" min="0" max="0" value="0"><b id="trade-year">${trade_year}</b></div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <caption>Reported goods trade. Latest static snapshot is ${trade_year}.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Traded good</th><th scope="col">World trade</th><th scope="col">Leading exporter</th><th scope="col">Boundary</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody id="trade-body">${trade_rows}</tbody>
  </table></div>
</div>

